# Ubuntu Determinant Alpha RS - Kernel Extensions
#

config USER_KO
	tristate "Per-User Kernel Objects (Memory Grain)"
	default m
	help
	  Allows users to load personal kernel objects according to a 3-tier
	  memory grain model:

	    Grain 1 (User Space):   Any user, max 4 MB
	    Grain 2 (Safety Space): Sudo rank 1+, max 16 MB
	    Grain 3 (Kernel Space): Sudo rank 4+, max 64 MB

	  Grain 1-2 do NOT trigger secure boot verification (sandbox path).
	  Grain 3 uses standard kernel module verification.

	  Admin interface: /proc/user_ko/{status, modules}

	  If unsure, say M.

config WHITE_ETHICS
	tristate "White Ethics Installer Grade"
	default m
	help
	  A system-level presence that covers the software in a careful aura
	  of elegance and future. The base user classes are protected by the
	  installer's position of status and even.

	  The system glows white for 2 hours every 8-36 hours (naturally
	  timed). During the glow, the system asserts its health, ethics,
	  and forward presence.

	  Properties: Careful, Brave, Heuristic, Elegant, Future-facing, Calming.

	  Admin interface: /proc/white_ethics/{status, glow}

	  If unsure, say M.

config CPUBOOST
	tristate "CPU Boost Designation"
	default m
	help
	  Per-process CPU frequency boost, designated by Grade 7+
	  administrators after program installation. Designated processes
	  run at boost frequency rather than power-conserving cycles.

	  Boost levels:
	    0 = OFF (normal governor)
	    1 = PREFER (soft hint)
	    2 = FORCE (pin to max base frequency)
	    3 = TURBO (beyond base max, if supported)

	  Designation requires Grade 7+ (sudo touch system) because boost
	  increases power consumption, thermal load, and affects hardware
	  longevity.

	  Admin interface: /proc/cpuboost/{status, list, designate, remove, toggle}

	  If unsure, say M.

config XGCC
	tristate "xgcc — Metal-Thin C/C++ Source Interpreter"
	default m
	help
	  A kernel-resident interpreter that takes .c, .h, .cpp, and .hpp
	  source files and executes them natively through a 4-model pipeline
	  without prior compilation to object code.

	  The Four Models:
	    1 = Basic Reduction (strip to executable semantics)
	    2 = Interrogative (evaluate conditional/questioning density)
	    3 = Iterative Suggest (optimize loops, predict bounds)
	    4 = Exact + Memory (literal execution with speed/category bounds)

	  Any single model would be adequate to produce a run. By default,
	  xgcc runs Model 3 and Model 4 together — the combined output runs.
	  Model 3 provides iterative optimization and bound prediction.
	  Model 4 provides exact fidelity with memory discipline (not too
	  ample in speed or category).

	  Device interface: /dev/xgcc (write source to execute)
	  Admin interface: /proc/xgcc/{status, models, memory}

	  If unsure, say M.
